course: dictionary
lesson: 8124
title: Pushing away: the word repel
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To repel something is to push it away or keep it away. «Opposite poles of two magnets attract and like poles repel.» Attract means pull together, so repel is the opposite: the magnets push each other apart.
explain: Repel has more than one meaning, so use the clues around it. «Insect repellent» is a spray that keeps mosquitoes away, and «waterproof» coats repel water, so the water is pushed off instead of soaking in.
explain: Repel can also mean to make someone feel disgusted. Read the whole sentence to check which meaning fits. If the thing is pushing something away physically, think magnets or water. If it is making someone feel sick or unwilling, think disgust.
fact: Repel means to push away or keep away.
fact: «Like poles repel each other» means two north poles, or two south poles, push each other apart.
fact: «Unlike poles attract» means a north pole and a south pole pull together.
fact: «Repel» can also mean to make someone feel disgusted.
fact: Pond skaters have «water-repelling hairs», so the water pushes away from their legs and they can stand on the surface.
exercise: «Opposite poles of two magnets attract and like poles repel.» What does repel mean here? | Stick firmly | Spin in a circle | Pull together | *Push each other apart
exercise: «Insect repellent spray keeps mosquitoes away.» What does repel mean in this sentence? | Warms up | *Keeps away | Invites in | Counts
exercise: Find and copy one word from «summoning, warning, inciting, repelling» that means pushing away. | inciting | *repelling | summoning | warning
test: Pond skaters have «water-repelling hairs». What does water-repelling mean? | Turns water into ice | Soaks up water | Makes water hot | *Pushes water away
test: A smell so bad that it makes you feel sick can repel you. What does repel mean here? | Calm | *Make disgusted | Surprise | Invite
exam: Two north poles of magnets are placed together. What happens? | Join | Attract | *Repel | Stick
